<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- Heddle declares ONE capability. Upstream declared seven.
         Under the hardened runtime an entitlement is what actually PERMITS access, so removing one
         means macOS denies the capability outright rather than leaving it merely unused. These were
         audited against the code before removal, not guessed at:
           device.camera                        no code references
           device.audio_input                   only agent voice input, which transcribes through
                                                ServerVoiceTranscriber against a server this fork
                                                does not have, and which is not compiled in at all
                                                because voice_input is not a default feature
           personal_information.addressbook     no code references
           personal_information.calendars       no code references
           personal_information.location        no code references
           personal_information.photos_library  no code references
         Each was removed together with its NSxxxUsageDescription in script/update_plist. The two
         must move together: an app that touches a protected resource while holding the entitlement
         but no usage string is terminated by the OS.
         Visible effect: Heddle no longer appears in six System Settings privacy panes.
         NOTE: XML comments may not contain a double hyphen. AMFI rejects the whole file if one
         appears here, while plutil lint accepts it, so a lint pass is not sufficient proof. -->

    <!-- Kept. A terminal runs the user's own programs, and macOS attributes a child process's
         request to the responsible app, so this is what lets osascript drive another application.
         That is an ordinary thing to run from a shell; without it such calls fail with error 1743.
         Heddle's own two uses would survive removal (cli_install spawns osascript for a shell
         script with administrator privileges, which is Authorization Services, and app.m only
         READS the current Apple event to find the quit reason). It is kept for the user's scripts,
         not for the app. -->
    <key>com.apple.security.automation.apple-events</key>
	<true/>

    <!-- The com.apple.security.application_groups entitlement was removed earlier, for a different
         reason: it requested an app group belonging to Warp's Apple team, and an app group must be
         owned by the signing team. Keeping it made the bundle unsignable by any other Developer ID
         and would fail notarization. Nothing depends on it:
         warp_core::paths::app_group_container_path was its only consumer, via secure_state_dir,
         and every caller already falls back to state_dir. -->
</dict>
</plist>
